WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.720
All right, friends.

00:04.720 --> 00:07.360
So now you might ask me why we are doing all those stuff.

00:07.360 --> 00:10.400
Why I would split my data by the columns.

00:10.400 --> 00:13.000
Well, it's all because of analytics.

00:13.000 --> 00:18.640
Because in analytics we have like big complex query where we have a lot of data aggregations and stuff

00:18.760 --> 00:19.880
on big tables.

00:19.880 --> 00:26.880
And the roster index is perfectly designed in order to improve the performance of such big queries.

00:26.920 --> 00:33.760
And that's why SQL databases like SQL server and as well BI tools like Tableau and Power BI did adopt

00:33.760 --> 00:38.760
this method in order to offer fast platform for data analysis.

00:38.760 --> 00:44.680
So now let's understand exactly why that Columnstore index is way faster for data analysis than the

00:44.680 --> 00:45.680
row store index.

00:45.680 --> 00:46.400
So let's go.

00:46.560 --> 00:48.280
So again we have the customers tables.

00:48.280 --> 00:51.800
And let's say we have like five customers where we have ID name and status.

00:51.800 --> 00:57.160
And as we learned before if you are using row store index the data can be stored in multiple databases.

00:57.160 --> 01:02.780
And in each database we can have the whole record, the whole information about one customer.

01:02.780 --> 01:05.580
So for this example we're going to have like three databases.

01:05.580 --> 01:09.780
But if you are using the Columnstore index it's going to be stored a little bit differently.

01:09.780 --> 01:12.540
So the first column the ID is going to be stored in one database.

01:12.660 --> 01:17.300
And here the SQL will not go and build a dictionary because the IDs are already short.

01:17.300 --> 01:20.380
So we're going to have like one data stream with all IDs.

01:20.420 --> 01:21.780
And now for the next column name.

01:21.780 --> 01:26.620
It's going to be stored in separate database where we're going to have an extra dictionary page where

01:26.620 --> 01:30.420
each name is going to be mapped to one small value so that data can be compressed.

01:30.420 --> 01:31.860
And we're going to save storage.

01:31.860 --> 01:36.300
Now the database is going to create for the third column the status one more data page.

01:36.300 --> 01:38.260
And the dictionary here is going to be very small.

01:38.260 --> 01:39.980
So for active we're going to have one.

01:39.980 --> 01:41.660
And for the inactive we're going to have two.

01:41.700 --> 01:45.700
And in the data stream we will be storing only the IDs of the dictionary.

01:45.700 --> 01:48.300
So now let's understand why the columnstore is faster.

01:48.300 --> 01:50.300
Let's have the following query.

01:50.340 --> 01:54.380
We want to find the total number of customers that are active.

01:54.380 --> 01:57.900
So we have the query select count star from customers.

01:57.900 --> 02:01.990
And we're going to filter the data by the status where it is equal to active.

02:02.110 --> 02:07.070
So now if we query the table with the row store, what can happen is we will have first to go and collect

02:07.070 --> 02:07.710
the data.

02:07.710 --> 02:12.110
So it's going to go to the first data page and collect the first two customer, then to the second to

02:12.190 --> 02:13.270
the third and so on.

02:13.270 --> 02:19.470
And as you can see SQL here is reading everything the whole row, the ID, the name, the status.

02:19.510 --> 02:23.750
Even though that for the query we actually don't need all those informations.

02:23.750 --> 02:28.030
We just need to count how many customers we need with the status active.

02:28.070 --> 02:32.310
But still SQL cannot go and selectively only reading the status.

02:32.630 --> 02:35.150
SQL has to read the whole record.

02:35.150 --> 02:38.230
So after SQL has all the data, it's going to go and filter the data.

02:38.230 --> 02:41.030
So it's going to go and remove the inactive rows.

02:41.070 --> 02:43.750
And then it's going to do the aggregate operation.

02:43.750 --> 02:46.150
And with that we're going to get three rows.

02:46.190 --> 02:49.470
So that's why the total count of active customers is going to be three.

02:49.510 --> 02:52.910
But now let's see how SQL can query the column store.

02:52.910 --> 02:57.810
So SQL first have to analyze okay which columns do I need actually for this query.

02:57.850 --> 03:00.130
Well, we need only the status.

03:00.130 --> 03:03.770
So SQL will not go and open all three data pages and read it.

03:03.930 --> 03:08.650
SQL will target only one data page, the data page where we have the column status.

03:08.650 --> 03:11.130
So it can take this very simple data stream.

03:11.130 --> 03:13.450
And then it can go and understand the dictionary.

03:13.450 --> 03:16.970
And it can go and remove all the values where it is equal to two.

03:17.050 --> 03:19.050
So without in the output we have only three values.

03:19.050 --> 03:22.770
And SQL going to go and do a very quick count for those values.

03:22.770 --> 03:27.050
So in the output we will get as well three total number of active customers.

03:27.050 --> 03:32.770
So now if you compare this intermediate result sets from the roster and the column store, you can see

03:32.770 --> 03:38.890
that in the row store we have fetched and retrieved a lot of unnecessary informations for this query.

03:38.930 --> 03:42.490
And this of course can make the speed of the query very slow.

03:42.490 --> 03:47.170
But in that column store SQL reads exactly what it needs for this aggregation.

03:47.170 --> 03:53.450
And we didn't read any extra informations about the names of the customers, the IDs it didn't like.

03:53.490 --> 03:55.170
Open any extra data pages.

03:55.170 --> 03:59.060
It exactly gets the data that it needs for the aggregation.

03:59.060 --> 04:05.340
And that's exactly why the performance of queries, where we have aggregations and data analysis is

04:05.340 --> 04:09.900
going to be very fast if you are using Columnstore compared to the row store.

04:09.980 --> 04:14.460
So that's why we use Columnstore for big data and data analytics.

04:14.500 --> 04:14.860
All right.

04:14.860 --> 04:19.860
So now let's summarize the differences between the roster and the columnstore indexes side by side.

04:19.980 --> 04:21.580
So let's start by the definition.

04:21.580 --> 04:24.980
The row store can go and organize and store the data row by row.

04:25.020 --> 04:28.460
It is really nice method if you need a lot of columns in one row.

04:28.500 --> 04:34.100
But in the other hand the Columnstore index can go and store the data and organize it column by column,

04:34.100 --> 04:36.980
which is really great if you're focusing on specific column.

04:37.020 --> 04:42.500
Now, if you are talking about the storage efficiency, the roster index can take more space compared

04:42.500 --> 04:43.940
to the Columnstore index.

04:43.940 --> 04:48.700
And that's because, as we learned, the column store can go and compress the data, which can save

04:48.740 --> 04:50.980
a lot of storage if you have large tables.

04:51.020 --> 04:56.760
Now to the next point, which is more important about the performance, the read and write optimizations.

04:56.760 --> 05:02.360
We can say for the roster, things are more balanced, so you will get a decent speed for both write

05:02.360 --> 05:03.720
and read operations.

05:03.720 --> 05:05.960
But things in the columnstore is different.

05:05.960 --> 05:12.040
It is fast for reading, especially if you are doing data analytics, but writing data like inserting

05:12.040 --> 05:18.600
and updating it is slower because as we learned, there are like multiple steps until the data is written

05:18.600 --> 05:19.680
in the pages.

05:19.680 --> 05:25.560
So in one hand you are optimizing the speed of your analytical queries, but in the other hand changing

05:25.560 --> 05:25.920
data.

05:25.920 --> 05:28.240
It is slower than the row store index.

05:28.280 --> 05:31.520
Now let's talk about the next point input and output efficiency.

05:31.520 --> 05:36.480
Well, the roaster index is not really good because you are retrieving a lot of columns.

05:36.480 --> 05:41.760
So a lot of data should be read from the disk storage in order to answer your queries.

05:41.760 --> 05:44.800
But in the other hand for the column store it is lower.

05:44.840 --> 05:50.200
And that's because it targets exactly the data and columns that is needed for the query.

05:50.200 --> 05:54.570
So there will be generally less data that is read from that disk storage.

05:54.570 --> 05:57.570
And of course that's why we are getting fast read performance.

05:57.570 --> 06:03.130
So now if you are thinking which systems are best for Rowstore index, well, the roster index is very

06:03.130 --> 06:10.010
suitable for the OLTP systems, online transactional systems like banking and commerce systems, where

06:10.010 --> 06:12.810
the full records are accessed very frequently.

06:12.810 --> 06:17.010
But on the other hand, the Columnstore index is great for OLAp.

06:17.050 --> 06:22.730
OLAp systems are online analytical processing where you have like data warehouses, data lake, business

06:22.730 --> 06:23.570
intelligence.

06:23.610 --> 06:25.930
You are building reports and analyses.

06:25.930 --> 06:30.130
You have large data sets and very complicated aggregated queries.

06:30.130 --> 06:34.290
So if you have such a project, then the Columnstore index is the way to go.

06:34.290 --> 06:37.370
So that means the use case for the Rowstore index.

06:37.370 --> 06:42.650
If you have high frequency transactions where the system has to quickly access records.

06:42.650 --> 06:48.770
And the use case for the Columnstore is big data analytics where the SQL has to scan large datasets.

06:48.810 --> 06:54.130
So those are the main differences between the Rowstore index and the Columnstore index.
